{"qid":"Q146246","names":["Kingdom of the Franks","Merovingian Empire","Carolingian Empire"],"title":"Kingdom of the Franks","endonym":{"native":"Francia","lang":"la"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of the Franks arose in the fifth century as Clovis I (r. 481–511) united the Salian Frankish tribes, defeated the last Roman commander Syagrius at Soissons in 486, crushed the Visigoths at Vouillé in 507, and converted to Catholic Christianity — securing papal and Romano-Gallic elite support for Frankish rule over most of ancient Gaul. The Merovingian dynasty that Clovis founded governed until 751, when the Carolingian mayor of the palace Pepin III deposed the last Merovingian and was crowned king with papal sanction. Under Charlemagne (r. 768–814) the kingdom expanded to encompass Saxony, northern Spain, Italy, and Bavaria, and was proclaimed the Carolingian Empire at Rome on Christmas Day 800; the dataset's terminal date of 849 reflects the post-843 Treaty of Verdun partition into East, Middle, and West Frankish successor kingdoms.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core Frankish territories in Gaul and the Rhineland are well attested by royal diplomas, capitularies, and ecclesiastical records from the sixth century onward; the extent of tributary or nominal Frankish authority over peripheral peoples such as Bretons and Basques is less precisely bounded.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Merovingian dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Merovingian-dynasty"},{"label":"Britannica: Carolingian dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Carolingian-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Francia",